# What EAS Build uploads. There is ALREADY an app/.easignore with the right
# intent — but it did not work, and this file is why it now does.
#
# eas.json lives in app/, but the git root is the repo root (here). EAS
# archived from the REPO ROOT, so app/.easignore's `android/` pattern was
# matched against `<repo>/android/` — which does not exist — instead of
# `<repo>/app/android/`, which is 2 GB. Result: a 563 MB upload containing a
# stale app-debug.apk, and a builder that emitted BOTH that debug APK and the
# release APK tarred together. The artifact came out as a .tar.gz that cannot
# be installed on a phone. That happened twice (2026-08-01, 2026-08-03).
#
# So: patterns here are relative to the REPO ROOT and must carry the `app/`
# prefix. app/.easignore is kept as-is in case EAS resolves the project root
# instead — whichever one wins, the heavy directories stay out.

# Dependencies — reinstalled on the builder.
node_modules/
**/node_modules/

# Native dirs — regenerated by prebuild on the builder.
app/android/
app/ios/

# Local build output and caches.
app/.expo/
app/dist/
app/web-build/
.expo/
dist/

# Never upload a built binary: a stray APK in the archive is exactly what makes
# the builder tar two artifacts together instead of emitting one .apk.
*.apk
*.aab
*.tar.gz

# Not part of the mobile build.
services/
website/
icon/
supabase/
.wrangler/
*.md

# Secrets never travel — EAS environment variables supply these
# (`eas env:list --environment preview`).
.env
.env.*
**/.env
**/.env.*
